%T A399557 250409,0,0,42461,357649,42409,0,0,881,29,10099,0,0,738781,461,257219,
%U A399557 0,0,11,104311,1459,0,0,2161,392159,379,0,0,54181,42409,1025459,0,0,1804841
%N A399557 a(n) is the smallest prime p such that p and p+2n are both terms of A179502, or 0 if no such p exists.
%C A399557 Every term of A179502 ends in 1 or 9.
%C A399557 So if p and p+2n are both terms: when 2n ends in 0, p and p+2n end in the same digit; when 2n ends in 2, p ends in 9 and p+2n ends in 1; when 2n ends in 8, p ends in 1 and p+2n ends in 9; and 2n cannot end in 4 or 6. Since 2n ends in 4 or 6 exactly when n == 2 or 3 (mod 5), a(n) = 0 for those n.
%C A399557 For every other n, a(n) > 0 would follow from Schinzel's Hypothesis H (the six polynomials p, (p^2+1)/2, (p^2+2)/3, p+2n, ((p+2n)^2+1)/2, ((p+2n)^2+2)/3 are irreducible, and no prime divides their product for every p: any prime >= 11 can exclude at most 10 residues of p, and 2, 3 and 7 never exclude all of them). a(n) > 0 for all such n <= 1000.
%C A399557 a(1) is the first term of A401114.
%H A399557 Mottaqui Karim, <a href="/A399557/b399557.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%e A399557 a(9) = 11: 11 and 11 + 18 = 29 are both terms of A179502, since 121, 122 = 2*61, 123 = 3*41 and 841, 842 = 2*421, 843 = 3*281 are all semiprimes, and 11 is the smallest term of A179502.
%e A399557 a(2) = 0 by the last-digit argument in the comments.
%o A399557 (Python)
%o A399557 from sympy import isprime, nextprime
%o A399557 def isA(k): return k > 3 and isprime(k) and isprime((k*k+1)//2) and isprime((k*k+2)//3)
%o A399557 def a(n):
%o A399557     if n % 5 in (2, 3): return 0
%o A399557     p = 5
%o A399557     while not (isA(p) and isA(p + 2*n)): p = nextprime(p)
%o A399557     return p
%Y A399557 Cf. A179502, A398403, A401114.
%K A399557 nonn,new
%O A399557 1,1
%A A399557 _Mottaqui Karim_, Nov 01 2026
